#import "main-defs.typ": *
#import "statements.typ": *
#import "diagrams/06-25-different-discriminant-global.typ": residue-towers

== Different, discriminant <sec:different-discriminant-global>

Throughout this section let $Lambda$ be a maximal $R$-order in the separable
$K$-algebra $A$, and let $tr : A -> K$ be the reduced trace map. By "ideal" we
mean, as usual, a full $R$-lattice in $A$. Given a two-sided $Lambda$-ideal $J$
in $A$, define its _complementary ideal_ as #idx("complementary ideal")
$
  tilde(J) = {x in A : tr x J subset R}.
$
Then $tilde(J)$ is also a two-sided $Lambda$-ideal in $A$. In particular, we
call $tilde(Lambda)$ the _inverse different_ of $Lambda$, and define the
_different_ of $Lambda$ as #idx("inverse different")#idx("different")
$
  frak(D) = frak(D)(Lambda slash R) = tilde(Lambda)^(-1).
$
Since $tilde(Lambda) supset Lambda$, it is clear that $frak(D)$ is a two-sided
ideal of $Lambda$. As in the proofs of @th:discriminant-local-and-basis and
@th:different-completion, it is easily verified that the process of forming
complementary ideals commutes with localization or completion at prime ideals of
$R$. Furthermore, as explained in Exercise~@exc:different-components, the
calculation of $frak(D)(Lambda slash R)$ can always be reduced to the central
simple case.

#theorem[
  Keeping the above notation, we have
  $
    tilde(J) = tilde(Lambda) dot J^(-1).
  $
] <th:complementary-ideal-inverse-different>

#proof[
  It suffices to prove the result locally, when $R$ is a discrete valuation
  #source(227)ring. But then $J = y Lambda$ for some $y in u(A)$, and so
  $tr x J = tr x y Lambda$. Thus $x in tilde(J)$ if and only if
  $x y in tilde(Lambda)$. Therefore
  $
    tilde(J) = tilde(Lambda) y^(-1) = tilde(Lambda) dot J^(-1),
  $
  which completes the proof.
]

The _discriminant_ $d(Lambda slash R)$ is the ideal of $R$ generated by
#idx("discriminant (ideal)")
$
  {det (tr x_i x_j)_(1 <= i, j <= n) : x_1, dots, x_n in Lambda},
$
where $n = (A : K)$. As in Exercises~@exc:discriminant-of-lattice
and~@exc:discriminant-base-change, forming discriminants commutes with
localization or completion at a prime ideal of $R$.

#theorem[
  $d(Lambda slash R) = N_(A slash K) (frak(D)(Lambda slash R))$.
] <th:discriminant-norm-different>

#proof[
  It suffices to prove the result locally, since the norm behaves properly under
  localization by @th:norm-local-principal. We have
  $
    N(frak(D)) = ord_R Lambda slash frak(D) = ord_R tilde(Lambda) slash Lambda
  $
  by Exercise~@exc:norm-inverse-ideal. Now let $Lambda = dsum_(i=1)^n R x_i$; as
  in the proof of @th:discriminant-local-and-basis, we have
  $
    d(Lambda slash R) = R dot det (tr x_i x_j)_(1 <= i, j <= n).
  $
  On the other hand, choose a dual basis ${y_j}$ of $A$ so that
  $tr x_i y_j = delta_(i j)$. Then $tilde(Lambda) = dsum R y_j$, and if we set
  $x_i = sum alpha_(i j) y_j$, $alpha_(i j) in K$, then
  $
    ord_R tilde(Lambda) slash Lambda = R dot det (alpha_(i j))
  $
  by Exercise~@exc:order-ideal-determinant. But $alpha_(i j) = tr x_i x_j$, so
  the proof is completed.
]

#theorem[
  The discriminant $d(Lambda slash R)$ is independent of the choice of the
  maximal order $Lambda$.
] <th:discriminant-independent-of-order>

#proof[
  Let $Gamma$ be another maximal $R$-order in $A$, and choose a normal ideal $L$
  in $A$ with left order $Gamma$ and right order $Lambda$. Then
  $Gamma = L Lambda L^(-1)$, and
  $frak(D)(Gamma) = L dot frak(D)(Lambda) dot L^(-1)$. Taking norms, we obtain
  $d(Gamma) = d(Lambda)$, as claimed.
]

We next prove the non-commutative analogue of @th:unramified-different.

#theorem(major: true)[
  Let $frak(P)$ be a prime ideal of the maximal $R$-order $Lambda$ in a
  separable $K$-algebra $A$. Set $P = frak(P) inter R$,
  $overline(R) = R slash P$, and write $P Lambda = frak(P)^e frak(Q)$, where
  $e >= 1$ and $frak(Q)$ is a two-sided ideal of $Lambda$ not divisible by
  $frak(P)$. Then $frak(P)^(e-1) divides frak(D)(Lambda slash R)$. If $e = 1$,
  then $frak(P) divides frak(D)(Lambda slash R)$ if and only if the center of
  the simple $overline(R)$-algebra $Lambda slash frak(P)$ is inseparable over
  $overline(R)$.
] <th:different-ramification>

#proof[
  _Step 1._ We show first that the proof can be reduced to the case where
  #source(228)$A$ is simple, and $R$ is a complete discrete valuation ring.
  Indeed, starting with the general case, let $hat(R)$ be the $P$-adic
  completion of $R$. If we replace each ideal by its $P$-adic completion, then
  the hypotheses are unchanged. (In this connection, note that from the relation
  $frak(P) + frak(Q) = Lambda$ it follows that
  $hat(frak(P)) + hat(frak(Q)) = hat(Lambda)$, so $hat(frak(P))$ does not divide
  $hat(frak(Q))$.) Since $frak(D)(hat(Lambda) slash hat(R))$ is the $P$-adic
  completion of $frak(D)(Lambda slash R)$, we know that
  $
    frak(P)^k divides frak(D)(Lambda slash R) quad "if and only if" quad
    hat(frak(P))^k divides frak(D)(hat(Lambda) slash hat(R)).
  $
  Hence it suffices to prove the desired result for the case where $R$ is
  complete.

  Changing notation, we assume for the remainder of this proof that $R$ is a
  complete discrete valuation ring. If we write $A$ as a direct sum
  $dsum_(i=1)^t A_i$ of simple components, then correspondingly
  $Lambda = dsum Lambda_i$, with $Lambda_i$ a maximal $R$-order in $A_i$.
  Renumbering the ${A_i}$ if need be, we may take
  $
    frak(P) = frak(P)_1 plus.o Lambda_2 plus.o dots.c plus.o Lambda_t,
  $
  where $frak(P)_1 = rad Lambda_1$ is the unique prime ideal of $Lambda_1$. Then
  $frak(Q)$ must have the form
  $
    frak(Q) = Lambda_1 plus.o frak(Q)_2 plus.o dots.c plus.o frak(Q)_t, quad
    frak(Q)_j = "two-sided ideal in" Lambda_j,
  $
  and likewise there is a decomposition
  $
    frak(D)(Lambda slash R) = frak(D)_1 plus.o dots.c plus.o frak(D)_t, quad
    frak(D)_i = frak(D)(Lambda_i slash R).
  $
  Then we have $P Lambda_1 = frak(P)_1^e$, and
  $frak(P)^k divides frak(D)(Lambda slash R)$ if and only if
  $frak(P)_1^k divides frak(D)_1$. Changing notation once more, we may thus
  assume for the remainder of the proof that $A$ is a simple separable
  $K$-algebra and that $frak(Q) = Lambda$, that is, $P Lambda = frak(P)^e$.

  _Step 2._ We now show that if $P Lambda = frak(P)^e$ then
  $frak(P)^(e-1) divides frak(D)$, where $frak(D) = frak(D)(Lambda slash R)$.
  Consider the $overline(R)$-algebra $overline(Lambda) = Lambda slash P Lambda$;
  since an $R$-basis of $Lambda$ maps onto an $overline(R)$-basis of
  $overline(Lambda)$, we may conclude (as in the proof of
  formula~@eq:char-pol-reduction) that
  $
    overline(charpol)_(A slash K) a
    = charpol_(overline(Lambda) slash overline(R)) overline(a), quad
    a in Lambda,
  $ <eq:char-pol-reduction-order>
  where bars denote reduction mod $P$. For each $a in frak(P)$ we have
  $overline(a)^e = 0$ in $overline(Lambda)$, and so $charpol overline(a)$ is a
  power of $X$. But then by @eq:char-pol-reduction-order, $charpol a$ (taken mod
  $P$) is a power of $X$. Since $charpol a$ is a power of $redcharpol a$ by
  @eq:semisimple-char-pol, we conclude that also $redcharpol a$ (taken mod $P$)
  is a power of $X$.

  But we have
  $
    redcharpol a = X^n - (tr a) X^(n-1) + dots.c + (-1)^n nr a,
  $
  and so we have now shown that
  $
    tr_(A slash K) frak(P) subset P.
  $
  Therefore $frak(P) subset P dot frak(D)^(-1)$, whence
  $frak(P)^(e-1) divides frak(D)$ as claimed.

  #source(229)_Step 3._ For the rest of the proof let $e = 1$, so that
  $P Lambda = frak(P)$ is the prime ideal of $Lambda$. Let $A = M_r (D)$, where
  $D$ is a skewfield with center $L$, and let $S$ be the integral closure of $R$
  in $L$. We set $(D : L) = n^2$, $(L : K) = k$. Then $Lambda$ is also a maximal
  $S$-order in $A$, and by @cor:complete-local-maximal-endomorphisms we may
  identify $Lambda$ with a full matrix ring $M_r (Delta)$ over the unique
  maximal $S$-order $Delta$ in $D$. From @cor:complete-local-radical and the
  formula
  $
    frak(P) = P Lambda = M_r (P Delta) = M_r (P S dot Delta),
  $
  it follows that $P S$ is the prime ideal of $S$, and $P Delta$ is the prime
  ideal of $Delta$. Therefore $overline(Delta) = Delta slash P Delta$ is a
  skewfield; since $Lambda slash frak(P) tilde.equiv M_r (overline(Delta))$, the
  skewfield $overline(Delta)$ has the same center $C$ as does the
  $overline(R)$-algebra $Lambda slash frak(P)$. Furthermore,
  $
    frak(D)(Lambda slash R) = M_r (frak(D)(Delta slash R))
  $
  by @th:different-matrix-order. Thus we need to prove that
  $P Delta divides frak(D)(Delta slash R)$ if and only if $C$ is inseparable
  over $overline(R)$, so we have now reduced the problem to the case of
  skewfields.

  _Step 4._ Keeping the notation of the preceding step, let $a in Delta$ map
  onto $overline(a) in overline(Delta)$, and let
  $
    f(X) = redcharpol_(D slash K) a, quad
    m(X) = minpol_(overline(R)) overline(a).
  $
  Then $f(X)$ has degree $n k$, whereas
  $
    "degree of" m(X) = (overline(R)(overline(a)) : overline(R)) = d,
  $
  say. Since $overline(Delta)$ is a skewfield, $m(X)$ must be irreducible over
  $overline(R)$, and hence
  $charpol_(overline(Delta) slash overline(R)) overline(a)$ is a power of
  $m(X)$. It then follows from @eq:char-pol-reduction-order that
  $overline(f)(X)$ is a power of $m(X)$, whence $d divides n k$. Furthermore, we
  obtain
  $
    overline(tr)_(D slash K) a = (n k slash d) dot T(overline(a)), quad
    a in Delta,
  $ <eq:reduced-trace-residue>
  where $T$ is the ordinary trace from the field $overline(R)(overline(a))$ to
  $overline(R)$.

  We shall now show that if $C$ ($=$ the center of $overline(Delta)$) is
  inseparable over $overline(R)$, then $overline(tr)_(D slash K) a = 0$ for each
  $a in Delta$. This will imply that $tr_(D slash K) Delta subset P$, whence
  $P^(-1) Delta subset frak(D)(Delta slash R)^(-1)$, and so
  $P Delta divides frak(D)(Delta slash R)$ as asserted. Let
  $p = op("char") overline(R)$, so $p in P$. If $overline(a)$ is inseparable
  over $overline(R)$ then $T(overline(a)) = 0$, so by @eq:reduced-trace-residue
  we obtain $overline(tr) a = 0$ as desired. On the other hand, suppose that
  $overline(a)$ is separable over $overline(R)$. Since $C$ is inseparable over
  $overline(R)$, there exists an element $b in Delta$ such that
  $overline(b) in C$ and $overline(b)$ is inseparable over
  $overline(R)(overline(a))$. Then $overline(R)(overline(a), overline(b))$ is a
  finite algebraic extension of $overline(R)$, so by the "Theorem of the
  primitive element" (see van der Waerden [@bib:Waerden1949]), there exists an
  element $c in Delta$ such that
  $overline(R)(overline(a), overline(b)) = overline(R)(overline(c))$. Therefore
  $
    (overline(R)(overline(c)) : overline(R))
    = (overline(R)(overline(a), overline(b)) : overline(R)(overline(a)))
    (overline(R)(overline(a)) : overline(R)) = (p^s d') dot d
  $
  for some $s >= 1$, where
  $(overline(R)(overline(a), overline(b)) : overline(R)(overline(a))) = p^s d'$
  and where $d = (overline(R)(overline(a)) : overline(R))$ as before. However,
  $(overline(R)(overline(c)) : overline(R))$ is the degree of
  $minpol_(overline(R)) overline(c)$, and thus divides $n k$ by the remarks in
  the preceding paragraph. This proves that $p^s dot d divides n k$, and so
  #source(230)$p^s divides (n k slash d)$. Therefore
  $overline(tr)_(D slash K) a = 0$ whenever $overline(a)$ is separable over
  $overline(R)$. We have thus established that
  $P Delta divides frak(D)(Delta slash R)$ whenever $C$ is inseparable over
  $overline(R)$.

  _Step 5._ Suppose finally that $C$ is separable over $overline(R)$, and that
  $e = 1$ as before. Since $P S$ is the prime ideal of $S$, it follows that
  $overline(S) = S slash P S$ is a field, and
  $(overline(S) : overline(R)) = (L : K) = k$ by @th:extensions-complete-fields.
  $
    #residue-towers
  $
  Further,
  $(overline(Delta) : overline(S)) = (Delta slash P Delta : overline(S))
  = (D : L) = n^2$.

  Set $(overline(Delta) : C) = m^2$; then
  $
    n^2 = (overline(Delta) : overline(S)) = m^2 (C : overline(S)),
  $
  so $(C : overline(S)) = t^2$ for some integer $t$. We show next that
  $C = overline(S)$, that is, $t = 1$. By @th:maximal-subfields there exists a
  maximal subfield $E$ of the skewfield $overline(Delta)$, with the property
  that $E$ is a separable extension of $C$. Then $E$ is a separable field
  extension of $overline(R)$, and so $E = overline(R)(overline(c))$ for some
  $c in Delta$. Since $(E : C) = m$ by @th:maximal-subfields, we obtain
  $
    (overline(R)(overline(c)) : overline(R))
    = (E : C)(C : overline(S))(overline(S) : overline(R)) = m t^2 k.
  $
  But on the other hand $(overline(R)(overline(c)) : overline(R))$ is a divisor
  of $n k$, by the first paragraph of Step~4. Thus $m t^2 k divides m t k$,
  whence $t = 1$ and $C = overline(S)$.

  It now follows that for each $a in Delta$, both of the polynomials
  $
    overline(redcharpol)_(D slash K) a, quad
    redcharpol_(overline(Delta) slash overline(R)) overline(a)
  $
  have degree $n k$. Both of them are powers of the irreducible polynomial
  $minpol_(overline(R)) overline(a)$, and hence they are equal. This shows that
  $
    overline(tr)_(D slash K) a = tr_(overline(Delta) slash overline(R))
    overline(a), quad a in Delta.
  $
  But by hypothesis $overline(Delta)$ is a separable $overline(R)$-algebra, and
  so there exists an element $a in Delta$ such that
  $tr_(overline(Delta) slash overline(R)) overline(a) != 0$. This shows that
  $tr_(D slash K) Delta subset.not P$, and hence it follows that
  $P Delta divides.not frak(D)(Delta slash R)$. This completes the proof of the
  theorem.
]

If $A$ is a central simple $K$-algebra, and $(A : K) = n^2$, then we have
$
  d(Lambda slash R) = N_(A slash K) (frak(D)(Lambda slash R)) = (nr frak(D))^n.
$
#source(231)We call $nr frak(D)$ the _ground ideal_ of $Lambda$. It is an ideal
of $R$, and is independent of the choice of the maximal order $Lambda$.
#idx("ground ideal")

For each prime ideal $P$ of $R$, the $P$-adic completion $hat(A)_P$ is
isomorphic to a full matrix algebra $M_(kappa_P) (S)$, where $S$ is a skewfield
with center $hat(K)_P$ and index $m_P$. We shall call $m_P$ the _local index_ of
$A$ at $P$, and $kappa_P$ the _local capacity_ of $A$ at $P$. #idx(
  "local index",
)#idx("local capacity")#idx("capacity")

Now suppose that $K$ is a global field. Then of course each residue class field
$R slash P$ is a finite field, and has no inseparable extensions. Hence for each
prime ideal $frak(P)$ of the maximal order $Lambda$, we have
$
  frak(P) divides frak(D)(Lambda slash R) quad "if and only if" quad
  frak(P)^2 divides P Lambda, quad "where" quad P = frak(P) inter R.
$
In fact, we have the more precise statement:

#theorem(major: true)[
  Let $A$ be a central simple $K$-algebra, where $K$ is a global field, and let
  $Lambda$ be a maximal $R$-order in $A$. For each $P$, let $m_P$ be the local
  index of $A$ at $P$, and let $frak(P)$ be the prime ideal of $Lambda$
  corresponding to $P$. Then $m_P = 1$ a.e., and
  $
    P Lambda = frak(P)^(m_P), quad
    frak(D)(Lambda slash R) = product_P frak(P)^(m_P - 1).
  $ <eq:different-global-field>
] <th:different-global-field>

#proof[
  Since $K$ is a global field, the first of the above equalities follows from
  @th:prime-factorization-maximal-order. As remarked at the start of this
  section, for each $P$ we have
  $
    frak(D)(hat(Lambda) slash hat(R))
    = hat(R) times.o_R frak(D)(Lambda slash R),
  $ <eq:different-completion-global>
  where $hat(R)$, $hat(Lambda)$ denote $P$-adic completions. The left hand
  expression equals $hat(frak(P))^(m_P - 1)$ by @th:local-different, since $K$
  is a global field. On the other hand, $frak(D)(Lambda slash R)$ is an ideal in
  $Lambda$, so $frak(D)_P = Lambda_P$ a.e., whence
  $frak(D)(hat(Lambda) slash hat(R)) = hat(Lambda)$ a.e. by
  @eq:different-completion-global. This shows that $m_P = 1$ a.e. It also
  establishes the second equality in @eq:different-global-field since that
  equality holds at each $P$-adic completion (see @th:lattices-completion).

  We may remark that in the above situation, the power of $frak(P)$ in
  $frak(D)(Lambda slash R)$ is precisely $frak(P)^(e-1)$, where
  $P Lambda = frak(P)^e$. Thus @th:different-ramification is in a sense best
  possible.
]

#corollary[
  Keeping the above notation, we have
  $
    nr frak(D)(Lambda slash R) = product_P P^((m_P - 1) kappa_P), quad
    d(Lambda slash R) = {nr frak(D)(Lambda slash R)}^n
  $
  where $n^2 = (A : K)$, and $kappa_P$ denotes the local capacity of $A$ at $P$.
] <cor:ground-ideal-discriminant>

#proof[
  Let $P$ be any prime ideal of $R$, $frak(P)$ the corresponding prime ideal of
  $Lambda$, and $M$ a maximal left ideal of $Lambda$ belonging to $frak(P)$.
  Then $nr M = P$ by @th:reduced-norm-maximal-ideal. On the other hand, the
  proof of @cor:norm-maximal-integral-ideal shows that
  $
    N(frak(P)) = N(M)^(kappa_P).
  $
  #source(232)Since the ordinary norm is the $n$th power of the reduced norm,
  this gives
  $
    nr frak(P) = P^(kappa_P).
  $ <eq:reduced-norm-prime-ideal>
  The formulas in @cor:ground-ideal-discriminant for the ground ideal
  $nr frak(D)$, and for the discriminant $d(Lambda slash R)$, now follow
  directly from @eq:different-global-field by taking reduced norms.
]

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none, outlined: false)[Exercises]

Let $Lambda$ be a maximal $R$-order in the separable $K$-algebra $A$.

#exercise[
  Using the notation of @th:maximal-orders-components, show that
  $
    frak(D)(Lambda slash R) = dsum frak(D)(Lambda_i slash R),
  $
  and that
  $
    frak(D)(Lambda_i slash R) = frak(D)(Lambda_i slash R_i) dot
    frak(D)(R_i slash R).
  $
  Here, $frak(D)(R_i slash R)$ is the different defined as in
  §~@ss:different-discriminant. #hint[@exc:different-components][Imitate the
    proof of Exercise~@exc:different-tower, using
    @eq:relative-trace-norm-transitivity.] <hint:different-components>
] <exc:different-components>

#exercise(tag: "1a")[
  Keeping the above notation, show that
  $
    d(Lambda slash R) = product d(Lambda_i slash R),
  $
  and that
  $
    d(Lambda_i slash R) = {N_(K_i slash K) d(Lambda_i slash R_i)}
    {d(R_i slash R)}^((A_i : K_i)),
  $
  where $d(R_i slash R)$ is the discriminant of $R_i$ with respect to $R$ (see
  §~@ss:different-discriminant). #hint[@exc:discriminant-components][By
    @th:discriminant-norm-different and Exercise~@exc:different-components,
    $
      d(Lambda slash R) = N_(A slash K) frak(D)(Lambda slash R)
      = product N_(A_i slash K) frak(D)(Lambda_i slash R)
      = product d(Lambda_i slash R).
    $
    Further,
    $
      d(Lambda_i slash R) = N_(A_i slash K) frak(D)(Lambda_i slash R)
      = {N_(A_i slash K) frak(D)(Lambda_i slash R_i)}
      {N_(A_i slash K) frak(D)(R_i slash R)}.
    $
    By Exercise~@exc:transitivity-trace-norm,
    $
      N_(A_i slash K) frak(D)(Lambda_i slash R_i)
      = N_(K_i slash K) {N_(A_i slash K_i) frak(D)(Lambda_i slash R_i)}
      = N_(K_i slash K) d(Lambda_i slash R_i).
    $
    Likewise
    $
      N_(A_i slash K) frak(D)(R_i slash R)
      & = N_(K_i slash K) {N_(A_i slash K_i) frak(D)(R_i slash R)} \
      & = {N_(K_i slash K) frak(D)(R_i slash R)}^((A_i : K_i))
      = {d(R_i slash R)}^((A_i : K_i)).
    $
    This yields the desired formulas for $d(Lambda slash R)$ and
    $d(Lambda_i slash R)$.] <hint:discriminant-components>
] <exc:discriminant-components>

#exercise[
  Let $M = M_(12)$ be a normal ideal of $A$, and define its _complementary
  ideal_ by #idx("complementary ideal")
  $
    tilde(M) = {x in A : tr x M subset R}.
  $
  Show that $tilde(M) = (tilde(M))_(21)$ is also a normal ideal of $A$, and that
  $tilde(tilde(M)) = M$ (compare Exercise~@exc:dual-lattice-form). Prove further
  that
  $
    tilde(M) = tilde(Lambda)_2 dot M^(-1) = M^(-1) dot tilde(Lambda)_1.
  $
  #source(233)\[Remark: Manipulations of this type can be used to give another
  proof of the fact that $O_l (M)$ is maximal if and only if $O_r (M)$ is
  maximal; see Deuring [@bib:Deuring1935, p.~84].\]
] <exc:complementary-normal-ideal>

#exercise[
  An element $a in u(A)$ is called an _$R$-unit_ if both $a$ and $a^(-1)$ are
  integral over $R$. Prove that if $a$ is integral over $R$, then $a$ is an
  _$R$-unit_ if and only if $N_(A slash K) a in u(R)$. #idx("unit")
  #hint[@exc:r-unit-norm][Let $a$ be integral over $R$, and let $Lambda$ be a
    maximal $R$-order in $A$ containing $R[a]$. If $N a in u(R)$, then
    $ord_R Lambda slash Lambda a = R$, so $Lambda = Lambda a$. Therefore
    $1 = x a$ for some $x in Lambda$, and clearly $x = a^(-1)$.]
  <hint:r-unit-norm>
] <exc:r-unit-norm>

#exercise[
  Let $Gamma subset Gamma'$ be $R$-orders in $A$, and let $x in Gamma$. Show
  that $x in u(Gamma)$ if and only if $x in u(Gamma')$. \[Remark: The result is
  clear from Exercise~@exc:r-unit-norm. Alternate proof (A.~Dress): if
  $x in Gamma inter u(Gamma')$, then $x^(-1)$ is integral over $R$, whence
  $x^(-1) in R[x] subset Gamma$; thus $x in u(Gamma)$.\]
] <exc:units-in-larger-order>
